22:26:51 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You’re absolutely right — and that’s a great observation. More and more modern shooters have moved away from hitscan and toward **visible, physically simulated projectiles** (with real travel time, bullet drop, and sometimes even wind). Games like *Battlefield*, *PUBG*, *Apex Legends*, *Warzone*, *Escape from Tarkov*, and many others all use projectile ballistics. Even series like *Call of Duty*, which historically used hitscan for most guns, now mix in projectile mechanics for certain modes or weapons. So the question becomes: if the bullet is a real, moving object that takes time to reach the target, how does the game make that work online, across different pings, without it feeling terrible? The answer expands on the “projectile” part I mentioned earlier, but let me break it down in much more detail. --- ### 1. The bullet you see is often a **predicted cosmetic proxy** on your screen When you pull the trigger, your client doesn’t wait for the server’s permission to show you a bullet. It immediately: - Calculates where the bullet should start based on your weapon’s barrel position and your current aim direction (adjusted for any lag compensation the client uses for its own view). - Spawns a visible tracer / projectile model. - Simulates its trajectory locally using the game’s ballistics rules (velocity, gravity, drag, etc.) for as long as the bullet is in flight. This gives you instant visual feedback, making the gun feel responsive. But here’s the important part: **your client’s simulation isn’t the authoritative truth**. It’s just a guess. The server will later tell you what really happened. --- ### 2. The server runs its own **authoritative simulation** at the same time When you fire, your client sends a message to the server containing: - The exact timestamp of when you pulled the trigger (based on your local clock, synchronized with the server). - Your precise aim angles. - Your character’s position, velocity, and other relevant data. The server then does its own simulation: - It **rewinds the game state** to the moment you fired (same lag compensation technique used for hitscan) to find out where you were *really* aiming at that instant, because the target might have moved between then and when the message arrived. - It spawns a **server-side projectile** from your barrel position with the exact same ballistics parameters. - Over multiple server ticks (often 30–60 Hz), the server moves the projectile forward, applying gravity, drag, etc., and checks for collisions with player hitboxes or world geometry. Because the server rewound time to the moment you pulled the trigger, the projectile’s starting direction is corrected for your latency. After that, the projectile flies forward through the “living” world on the server — meaning **if a target moves after you fire, they can actually dodge the bullet**, even though on your screen it looked like it was heading right at them. --- ### 3. Reconciling your client’s prediction with the server’s result Once the server’s projectile either hits something or expires (fades out), the server sends your client an **authoritative event**: - If the bullet hit a player: server sends “you hit enemy X in the head for 45 damage.” - If the bullet missed everything: server sends “your bullet traveled to position Y and then disappeared.” Now your client reconciles: - If your local predicted bullet hit the same target at a similar time/location, all is well. The client simply attaches the real damage numbers to what you saw. - If your prediction was wrong (e.g., on your screen you saw the bullet hit an enemy, but on the server the enemy had already ducked behind cover), the server’s version overrides. Your client **destroys the cosmetic bullet, removes any fake blood effects, and corrects the outcome**. This can cause the infamous “no-reg” or “behind cover” kill/death scenarios — you saw a hit, but the server says no. To reduce these discrepancies, the client’s ballistics simulation must match the server’s *exactly* — same physics constants, same tick timing, same random seeds if there’s any spread. That way, in most normal situations, the prediction and the server’s outcome are nearly identical. --- ### 4. What about the bullet you see from another player’s perspective? When *another* player fires, you don’t need to see the bullet instantly. That player’s client sends its fire event to the server, the server simulates the projectile, and then the server broadcasts the projectile’s state (or just its creation and destruction events) to all other clients. Your client receives a message like “Player A fired a bullet from position X with velocity V at time T.” Your client then: - Uses **interpolation** to smooth out the bullet’s motion, since the server updates it at a fixed tick rate. - Since your view of Player A is already slightly delayed (by the interpolation buffer), the server can feed you the bullet trajectory without needing heavy lag compensation for the visual. You simply see the projectile fly from where Player A’s interpolated character was at the time they fired, which is consistent with what the server recorded. Some games opt to send the full bullet trajectory as a spline or spawn a visual-only tracer that follows the server-determined path, reducing bandwidth. --- ### 5. Why not just simulate the bullet entirely on the client? Because that would be trivially cheatable. A client could simply tell the server “I hit that guy 500 meters away while spinning in a circle.” By keeping the simulation server-authoritative, the game ensures that **only the server decides hits**. The visible bullet on your screen is just a pretty, responsive visual – the real projectile is the invisible one on the server. --- ### 6. Putting it all together in a typical online projectile shooter (e.g., *Battlefield*) 1. You press fire. Your client immediately shows a tracer darting out of your gun, arcing with drop, heading toward a distant sniper. 2. At the same time, your client sends the fire event (timestamp, aim angles) to the server. 3. The server rewinds to that timestamp, checks your exact aim against the target’s past position, and spawns a server-side projectile from your corrected barrel. 4. The server advances the projectile each tick, checking for collision against the current (not rewound) positions of players and world objects. 5. 200 ms later, the server’s projectile hits the sniper’s head. The server sends you a “hit” confirmation. 6. Your client, which already predicted that hit (since the sniper was standing still), playes the headshot sound and shows the death. Everything lines up. 7. If the sniper had moved, your client would have shown a miss or a hit that suddenly gets corrected — but because the server did the real collision, the outcome is always fair (you missed, because he moved before the bullet arrived, just like in real life). --- So the key takeaway: **visible bullet travel is real simulation, but it’s split into two parallel worlds.** Your client simulates a prediction for instant visual feedback, while the server runs the authoritative simulation, rewinding time at the moment of fire to compensate for your latency, then letting the bullet fly forward in true server time. Your client’s display is only a visual helper; the server’s invisible bullet is what actually counts.
